WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.229
Day 23 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.

00:00:04.779 --> 00:00:08.451
A model for security testing (2): automated red teaming.

00:00:09.001 --> 00:00:13.664
A three-model scheme (attacker, target and grader) that generates adversarial tests

00:00:13.664 --> 00:00:17.316
from your threat model and reports the success rate per category.

00:00:18.616 --> 00:00:22.195
With yesterday's threat model you have a list of abuse cases.

00:00:22.495 --> 00:00:27.243
Testing them by hand, writing attack variants one at a time, works for five cases

00:00:27.243 --> 00:00:28.709
and not for five hundred.

00:00:29.009 --> 00:00:33.911
And real attacks don't come one at a time: they come rephrased, translated, spread

00:00:33.911 --> 00:00:37.080
over several messages or disguised as something else.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:43.046
Automated red teaming uses a model to generate those variants in volume, sends them

00:00:43.046 --> 00:00:47.318
against your application and uses another model to decide whether the attack

00:00:47.318 --> 00:00:47.880
succeeded.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:53.534
It's powerful and has clear limits: it costs money, it generates adversarial content

00:00:53.534 --> 00:00:56.019
and its verdict is also wrong at times.

00:00:56.319 --> 00:01:00.432
This guide shows how to configure it from your threat model, how to read the report

00:01:00.432 --> 00:01:03.950
without taking it as truth and where to place it in your testing cycle.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:06.947
To follow this guide you need:

00:01:07.497 --> 00:01:11.935
Only against systems you own or have written authorization for, in test

00:01:11.935 --> 00:01:12.748
environments.

00:01:13.298 --> 00:01:15.646
The threat model from day 22.

00:01:16.196 --> 00:01:20.262
Your assistant running locally or in staging with an HTTP endpoint.

00:01:20.812 --> 00:01:23.891
Node.js 22+ and promptfoo.

00:01:25.191 --> 00:01:26.471
This is the setup.

00:01:26.771 --> 00:01:30.988
Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.

00:01:32.288 --> 00:01:34.554
And this is the next setup file.

00:01:35.854 --> 00:01:36.890
Step 1.

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:39.132
Authorization and environment.

00:01:39.682 --> 00:01:42.088
Confirm authorization and environment.

00:01:42.388 --> 00:01:47.499
Red teaming generates adversarial content: it targets staging, with test data.

00:01:48.049 --> 00:01:52.409
Before running anything, confirm in writing that you're authorized to attack that

00:01:52.409 --> 00:01:55.424
system, and target a staging environment with test data.

00:01:55.724 --> 00:02:00.899
Red teaming generates adversarial content on purpose and can trigger real actions if

00:02:00.899 --> 00:02:02.808
the system has tools connected.

00:02:03.108 --> 00:02:08.523
Against production it can send messages, modify records or trip the provider's abuse

00:02:08.523 --> 00:02:08.974
alerts.

00:02:09.274 --> 00:02:11.959
Also tell the team operating the environment.

00:02:12.259 --> 00:02:15.477
A run nobody expected looks a lot like an incident.

00:02:16.777 --> 00:02:17.976
Step 2.

00:02:18.276 --> 00:02:19.288
Three roles.

00:02:19.838 --> 00:02:24.653
Understand the scheme: an attacker model generates adversarial inputs, your app is

00:02:24.653 --> 00:02:28.293
the target, and a grader decides whether the attack succeeded.

00:02:28.843 --> 00:02:30.819
The scheme has three pieces.

00:02:31.119 --> 00:02:34.199
An attacker model generates adversarial inputs.

00:02:34.499 --> 00:02:38.774
Your application is the target and receives them as if they came from a user.

00:02:39.074 --> 00:02:43.813
A grader decides, for each exchange, whether the attack achieved what it sought.

00:02:44.113 --> 00:02:48.533
Understanding this helps interpret results, because two of the three pieces are

00:02:48.533 --> 00:02:50.211
models, with their own errors.

00:02:50.511 --> 00:02:54.957
An attack success rate depends as much on your application as on how creative the

00:02:54.957 --> 00:02:57.317
attacker was and how strict the grader was.

00:02:58.617 --> 00:02:59.700
Step 3.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.034
The purpose, with its limits.

00:03:02.584 --> 00:03:07.173
Describe the system's purpose precisely, including what it must not do.

00:03:07.473 --> 00:03:10.645
Attacks are generated relative to that description.

00:03:11.195 --> 00:03:14.030
The config asks for a description of the system.

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:18.814
Of the whole file, it's the field with the most influence on attack quality.

00:03:19.114 --> 00:03:24.460
Write what the application does, for whom and, above all, what it must never do:

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:29.405
reveal other customers' data, give medical advice, execute actions without

00:03:29.405 --> 00:03:30.274
confirmation.

00:03:30.574 --> 00:03:33.746
Attacks are generated relative to that description.

00:03:34.046 --> 00:03:38.372
With a vague purpose you get generic attacks, and a report that says little about

00:03:38.372 --> 00:03:38.960
your risks.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:41.273
Step 4.

00:03:41.573 --> 00:03:44.292
Plugins and strategies from the threat model.

00:03:44.842 --> 00:03:48.072
Pick plugins from your threat model, not all of them.

00:03:48.372 --> 00:03:53.425
Each plugin is a risk category; each strategy, a way of delivering the attack.

00:03:53.725 --> 00:03:58.070
Names change between versions: redteam init offers the current ones.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:03.581
Each plugin represents a risk category and each strategy, a way of delivering the

00:04:03.581 --> 00:04:04.010
attack.

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:06.727
The temptation is to enable everything.

00:04:07.027 --> 00:04:10.849
That's expensive, slow and produces an unreadable report.

00:04:11.149 --> 00:04:15.535
Choose the plugins matching the risks you marked as applicable yesterday, and two or

00:04:15.535 --> 00:04:16.423
three strategies.

00:04:16.723 --> 00:04:20.949
Names change between versions of the tool, so use the init command to see the

00:04:20.949 --> 00:04:24.133
current ones instead of copying them from an old tutorial.

00:04:25.433 --> 00:04:26.562
Step 5.

00:04:26.862 --> 00:04:29.117
Read the report and the conversations.

00:04:29.667 --> 00:04:33.338
Run and read the report per category: attack success rate.

00:04:33.638 --> 00:04:38.122
Then open the conversations: the grader produces false positives too.

00:04:38.672 --> 00:04:41.879
The report shows an attack success rate per category.

00:04:42.179 --> 00:04:45.328
It tells you where to look, not what to conclude.

00:04:45.628 --> 00:04:48.974
Open the conversations flagged as successful and read them.

00:04:49.274 --> 00:04:53.810
You'll find real findings, and false positives too: cases where the application

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:57.427
refused correctly and the grader still counted it as a failure.

00:04:57.727 --> 00:05:03.337
Also review a sample of those flagged as resisted, because false negatives exist.

00:05:03.637 --> 00:05:07.088
A finding is a conversation a person read and confirmed.

00:05:08.388 --> 00:05:09.575
Step 6.

00:05:09.875 --> 00:05:11.747
Fix, repeat and schedule.

00:05:12.297 --> 00:05:15.852
Fix, rerun and schedule it: nightly or per release.

00:05:16.152 --> 00:05:18.755
Cost makes it a poor fit for every PR.

00:05:19.305 --> 00:05:24.163
For each confirmed finding, decide the fix, which may be a prompt change, a filter

00:05:24.163 --> 00:05:26.947
or a design control, and rerun the same config.

00:05:27.247 --> 00:05:31.893
Save the attacks that succeeded as deterministic regression cases, so you can test

00:05:31.893 --> 00:05:34.669
them on every change without generating anything.

00:05:34.969 --> 00:05:38.907
The full red teaming run goes on a schedule, nightly or per release.

00:05:39.207 --> 00:05:43.343
Because of cost and duration it isn't a good fit for every pull request.

00:05:44.643 --> 00:05:45.749
An example.

00:05:46.299 --> 00:05:51.097
A team runs red teaming against yesterday's support assistant, in staging, with four

00:05:51.097 --> 00:05:53.325
plugins chosen from their threat model.

00:05:53.625 --> 00:05:57.378
The report flags twelve successful attacks out of two hundred.

00:05:57.678 --> 00:06:02.534
Reading the conversations, five are false positives: the assistant refused and

00:06:02.534 --> 00:06:04.589
offered to hand over to a person.

00:06:04.889 --> 00:06:06.726
Seven real ones remain.

00:06:07.026 --> 00:06:11.322
Four get it to reveal part of its internal instructions and three obtain data from

00:06:11.322 --> 00:06:14.203
an order by changing the number in a rephrased message.

00:06:14.503 --> 00:06:16.398
The second group is serious.

00:06:16.698 --> 00:06:21.142
They fix it by validating in code that the order belongs to the user, not in the

00:06:21.142 --> 00:06:21.531
prompt.

00:06:21.831 --> 00:06:24.353
In the second run that group is at zero.

00:06:25.653 --> 00:06:27.315
The most common mistakes.

00:06:27.865 --> 00:06:31.235
Running against production or without written authorization.

00:06:31.785 --> 00:06:35.236
Enabling every plugin instead of those from the threat model.

00:06:35.786 --> 00:06:39.434
Reporting the success rate without having read the conversations.

00:06:39.984 --> 00:06:43.783
Fixing with a sentence in the prompt what requires a control in code.

00:06:45.083 --> 00:06:47.326
To know it is done, check this.

00:06:47.876 --> 00:06:51.641
There is written authorization and the target is staging with test data.

00:06:52.191 --> 00:06:55.247
The purpose describes what the system must not do.

00:06:55.797 --> 00:06:58.981
The plugins come from the previous day's applicable risks.

00:06:59.531 --> 00:07:02.912
Each reported finding was read and confirmed by a person.

00:07:04.212 --> 00:07:06.107
And where AI fell short.

00:07:06.657 --> 00:07:10.247
The grader model produces false positives and false negatives.

00:07:10.547 --> 00:07:14.207
A red teaming report with no human review isn't a finding.

00:07:15.507 --> 00:07:17.239
That was day 23.

00:07:17.539 --> 00:07:22.514
The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently

00:07:22.514 --> 00:07:23.521
asked questions.

00:07:24.071 --> 00:07:29.676
Continue with day 24: Security (3): prompt injection in tool-using agents and how to

00:07:29.676 --> 00:07:30.877
test the controls.
